    23	Clipping is one rule based on states and widths, whatever the CSS pattern: it applies to text and controls only. Cropped media is never clipped content: an image cropped by its frame, an image centred in a `picture`, a hover zoom, a round avatar crop, a cropped video, a decorative SVG or shape that bleeds out of its section, `object-fit` and background images all pass; text or a control inside a cropped media wrapper is still checked, and a box that cuts nothing (or only media and decoration) is not a clip. Content Chrome does not render is not on screen: the content of a closed `<details>` (a mobile menu), `display:none`, `content-visibility: hidden` and closed popovers are not measured, and their open state is graded only when it is declared. The fallback content inside `video`, `audio`, `object` and `canvas` is not rendered either, so it is not text on screen (the element itself is media, or a nested document). Links and named controls are content in their own right, by their box and accessible name: an icon-only link (`aria-label`, or an image's alt text), a checkbox named by `aria-labelledby`, a button. Content is matched by a typed print: text by its text (`text:`), a control by its role and accessible name (`button:Subscribe`, `link:Your profile`); text matches only text, so a heading with the same words never stands in for a lost button. Text inside a control belongs to the control, so a control whose shown label shortens at a width is matched by its name: it is not lost when its accessible name stays the same, or when its shown name is a prefix or abbreviation of its 1440 name ("Demo" for "Request a demo"); a link left with no name (a wordmark whose text is hidden) is lost. (1) Any text or control shown at 1440 in a state must be fully shown, or reachable by scrolling, at every declared width, 320 included, in that state, or it fails (a table whose columns an `overflow:hidden` wrapper cuts at 320, nav links cut by a nowrap bar); text or a control sho